Ray Dillinger wrote:

 > Mathematically this is sorta related to the Kelly
 > criterion
 > (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kelly_criterion) for
 > making an optimal bet.

 > Every turn, you need to pick the action with the
 > highest marginal return of survival odds.

It's important for the case of NetHack to notice
that the optimal action in NetHack (considered as a
game whose goal is ascending) is _not_ the optimal
action to let you survive the immediate situation,
but the optimal action to promote ascension,
_including_ consideration of the current situation.

Thus, searching 50 times on each space may lessen
your chances of landing in a trap more than
searching one time on each space, but it heightens
your chances of dying to the extra monsters
generated while you dither, or of starving because
you dithered, or of dying much later because you
forgot a spell that you'd still know if you hadn't
dithered so many turns away searching.

